    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d787">

                <pb facs="1370" n="1356" sameAs="frus:frus1950v06/pg1356"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Lot 56D527</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Director of the Office of Northeast
                        Asian Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Allison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Consultant to the Secretary</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dulles</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-12-07">December 7, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Japanese Peace Treaty<anchor n="1" xml:id="d787-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>There is attached a rather hastily dictated memorandum setting forth some of the
                    more urgent matters I believe we should consider in connection with our next
                    step toward a Japanese peace settlement. The attached memorandum is not in any
                    sense a complete statement of all factors to be taken into account, and as I
                    read it over I can see obvious omissions. However, I am forwarding it in the
                    hope that it will be suggestive and that it may elicit comments which will help
                    us to make progress.</p>


                <xi:include href="d787-a1.xml" xpointer="d787-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d787-f1" n="1" xml:id="d787-f1-content">
                <p>In his memorandum of the Secretary’s morning meeting of December 7, Mr.
                    McWilliams stated in part: “Mr. Rusk reported on the Japanese Peace Treaty. He
                    said that we still have hopes of pursuing the treaty but we are considering two
                    steps in the interim to improve the situation. The steps are: (1) transfer of
                    more authority and responsibility to the Japanese; and (2) a declaration ending
                    the war with Japan which would be followed by a treaty of friendship, commerce
                    and navigation.” (Executive Secretariat Files)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
